1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:05,892
This is greenroom. A chat window into a code repository, for someone who does not code. This

2
00:00:05,892 --> 00:00:10,884
film shows what they see, and then what happens underneath.

3
00:00:12,784 --> 00:00:17,579
I built a website for someone who is not technical. Every small change still came back to me.

4
00:00:17,579 --> 00:00:22,373
Make the headline bigger. Change the opening hours. Each one was five minutes of my time and

5
00:00:22,373 --> 00:00:27,168
a day of theirs waiting. I wanted them to make the change themselves, see it, and hand it to

6
00:00:27,168 --> 00:00:31,963
me for a quick look before it went live.

7
00:00:33,863 --> 00:00:38,919
This is what they see. A chat on the left, a live preview of their site on the right. A few

8
00:00:38,919 --> 00:00:43,975
suggestions of things to say, written for this site. Nothing to install and no password. Their

9
00:00:43,975 --> 00:00:49,031
phone is on a private network, and that is the login.

10
00:00:50,531 --> 00:00:55,203
They tap a suggestion and press Send. Underneath, Claude Code is now working inside a private

11
00:00:55,203 --> 00:00:59,875
copy of the site. The short lines in the chat are its actions: looking at a file, changing it,

12
00:00:59,875 --> 00:01:04,547
checking the site still builds.

13
00:01:07,047 --> 00:01:12,657
Half a minute later it replies in plain words. The preview on the right already shows the new

14
00:01:12,657 --> 00:01:18,268
opening hours. Nothing has gone live. The change exists only in the private copy.

15
00:01:18,968 --> 00:01:23,263
When they are happy, they press Send to Alex. Alex is me, the person who looks after the site.

16
00:01:23,263 --> 00:01:27,558
They can add a note. What comes out the other end is a pull request, and the playground resets

17
00:01:27,558 --> 00:01:31,853
for the next idea.

18
00:01:38,253 --> 00:01:44,152
Here is the whole thing on one page. The phone reaches my machine over Tailscale. greenroom

19
00:01:44,152 --> 00:01:50,051
is one Node file with no dependencies. It runs Claude Code in one-shot mode inside a git worktree

20
00:01:50,051 --> 00:01:55,949
of the repo, with a tool list that allows reading, editing and building, and nothing else. The

21
00:01:55,949 --> 00:02:01,848
preview is the normal dev server. Send pushes a branch and opens the pull request.

22
00:02:03,972 --> 00:02:09,455
Now the same request from underneath, in a terminal on my machine. greenroom runs as a user

23
00:02:09,455 --> 00:02:14,937
service. Inside it, one node process and the dev server for the preview.

24
00:02:17,232 --> 00:02:21,733
The service log shows the turn. Who asked, which model answered, and how long it took. Then

25
00:02:21,733 --> 00:02:26,235
the send: a branch pushed and a pull request opened.

26
00:02:28,475 --> 00:02:33,723
The private copy is a git worktree. Same repository, its own branch, its own installed dependencies.

27
00:02:33,723 --> 00:02:38,971
Claude Code never touches the checkout I work in.

28
00:02:42,969 --> 00:02:47,598
The pull request carries the request in the person's own words, the files that changed, and

29
00:02:47,598 --> 00:02:52,228
a label for the review tier. This one touched only the copy file, so it is words. I read it

30
00:02:52,228 --> 00:02:56,857
on my phone and merge it, or not.

31
00:02:59,320 --> 00:03:04,276
Every send is also written to a decisions file with the request, the files and the tier. When

32
00:03:04,276 --> 00:03:09,233
I merge or close the pull request, greenroom records that too. In time that file trains a small

33
00:03:09,233 --> 00:03:14,189
model to predict which changes need a look.

34
00:03:15,850 --> 00:03:19,370
Every turn logs its tokens and its cost, per person. So I know what each user is spending, and

35
00:03:19,370 --> 00:03:22,890
on which session.

36
00:03:25,094 --> 00:03:30,214
The tiers come from the files a change touched. Words for copy and images, content for data,

37
00:03:30,214 --> 00:03:35,334
design for styles and pages, code for anything else. Words can be set to merge on its own once

38
00:03:35,334 --> 00:03:40,454
the checks pass. I keep that off for now.

39
00:03:42,749 --> 00:03:47,656
Which model answers is one word in a file, read on every message. Claude, or any open model

40
00:03:47,656 --> 00:03:52,562
that speaks the same format, hosted or running on this machine. And the allow list is a text

41
00:03:52,562 --> 00:03:57,469
file of logins, one per line.

42
00:04:02,148 --> 00:04:06,809
I measured eleven models on the same request. The hosted open models did the job in under a

43
00:04:06,809 --> 00:04:11,471
minute for a fraction of a cent. The local models that fit a twelve gigabyte card are not there

44
00:04:11,471 --> 00:04:16,132
yet. The sandbox and the pull request are the same whichever one is running. What changes is

45
00:04:16,132 --> 00:04:20,793
judgement.

46
00:04:22,793 --> 00:04:27,391
That is greenroom. One file, a private network, a worktree, and a pull request. The code and

47
00:04:27,391 --> 00:04:31,988
the write-up are linked below. Thanks for watching.

